# EPISODE 009: The Infancy Gospel of Thomas — stories of Jesus between the ages of five and twelve.
# Each entry: (kind, text, visual)
# Quotes (public domain only): "The Gospel of Thomas, First Greek Form", tr. Alexander Walker, Ante-Nicene Fathers vol. 8
# (1886; Buffalo printing 1887); Irenaeus, Against Heresies 1.20.1 (tr. Roberts & Rambaut, ANF vol. 1, 1885);
# John Chrysostom, Homily 17 on John (Library of the Fathers, Oxford, 1848); KJV (Luke 2:52, John 2:11).
# Modern translations (Hock, Burke, Ehrman) are copyrighted and are NOT quoted.

S = [
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- COLD OPEN
 ("vo", "A five year old boy kneels by a stream, shaping twelve sparrows out of mud. It is the Sabbath. A neighbor runs to tell his father. The boy claps his hands, and the birds fly away.", "rh_134"),
 ("vo", "Later, another boy bumps into him in the street, and falls down dead. And three different teachers try to teach him the alphabet. None of them succeeds.", "tis_77"),
 ("vo", "The boy is Jesus. The book is not in the Bible. It is the Infancy Gospel of Thomas, and Christians read it, from Ireland to Ethiopia, for more than a thousand years.", "tis_74"),
 ("vo", "And in 2024, its oldest known copy turned up in a library in Hamburg, on a scrap of papyrus no one had recognized.", "tisch_241"),
 ("title", "THE INFANCY GOSPEL OF THOMAS|Jesus as a Boy", "dore_061"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- I
 ("chapter", "I|The Silent Years", "rh_131"),
 ("vo", "The Gospels of Matthew and Luke tell of a birth in Bethlehem, a star, and a flight into Egypt. Then the story goes almost silent.", "dore_059"),
 ("vo", "Luke gives a single scene, Jesus at twelve in the Temple, and one sentence to cover the years that follow.", "tis_74"),
 ("vo", "In the words of the King James Bible: And Jesus increased in wisdom and stature, and in favour with God and man.", "tis_77"),
 ("vo", "Then nothing, until he is about thirty years old. For early Christians, that silence was an invitation. What was he like as a child? Did he already have his power? Did he know who he was?", "rh_131"),
 ("vo", "By the second century, storytellers had begun to answer. The collection we call the Infancy Gospel of Thomas follows Jesus from the age of five to twelve.", "tisch_241"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- II
 ("chapter", "II|Sparrows of Clay", "rh_134"),
 ("vo", "The book opens beside a mountain stream. The boy gathers the running water into little pools, and makes it clear with a word. Then he takes the soft clay from the bank.", "rh_134"),
 ("vo", "Out of it he fashions twelve sparrows. And it is the Sabbath. A man sees it and reports to Joseph that his son has broken the Sabbath. Joseph comes and scolds him.", "rh_134"),
 ("quote", "And Jesus clapped His hands, and cried out to the sparrows, and said to them: Off you go! And the sparrows flew, and went off crying.|The Gospel of Thomas, First Greek Form 2 (tr. A. Walker, Ante-Nicene Fathers vol. 8, 1886)", "dore_067"),
 ("vo", "This is the most famous story in the book, and it traveled far. The Quran, in the seventh century, also tells of Jesus shaping a bird from clay and breathing life into it, by God's permission.", "dore_058"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- III
 ("chapter", "III|A Dangerous Child", "rh_137"),
 ("vo", "What comes next has puzzled readers for centuries. The son of Annas the scribe takes a willow branch and drains the boy's pools. Jesus is angry. He tells the boy he will wither like a tree, and the boy withers.", "rh_137"),
 ("vo", "Soon after, a child runs into Jesus and strikes his shoulder. Jesus says, you will not go back the way you came, and the child falls dead.", "rh_131"),
 ("vo", "The parents go to Joseph. Either leave the village, they say, or teach him to bless, and not to curse, for he is killing our children.", "tis_38"),
 ("vo", "When Joseph rebukes him, the people who complained are struck blind. And Joseph takes the boy by the ear, and pulls it hard.", "tis_38"),
 ("vo", "It is a startling picture: a child with the power of God, and the temper of a child.", "tis_77"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- IV
 ("chapter", "IV|Alpha and Beta", "dore_066"),
 ("vo", "A teacher named Zacchaeus overhears the boy, and offers to teach him his letters. He writes out the alphabet. The boy recites it, from Alpha to Omega, and then turns on him: if you do not know the nature of the Alpha, how can you teach the Beta?", "dore_066"),
 ("vo", "He lectures the old man on the lines and strokes of the first letter. Zacchaeus gives up.", "dore_061"),
 ("quote", "That child does not belong to this earth; he can tame even fire. I made a struggle to have a scholar, and I was found to have a teacher.|The Gospel of Thomas, First Greek Form 7 (tr. A. Walker, Ante-Nicene Fathers vol. 8, 1886)", "dore_061"),
 ("vo", "This little scene is our earliest clue to the book's age. Around the year one hundred and eighty, Irenaeus of Lyon complained that a group he opposed, the followers of a teacher named Marcus, used a false story.", "dore_066"),
 ("quote", "But when, again, the teacher bade Him say, Beta, the Lord replied, Do thou first tell me what Alpha is, and then I will tell thee what Beta is.|Irenaeus, Against Heresies 1.20.1 (tr. A. Roberts & W. H. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers vol. 1, 1885)", "dore_061"),
 ("vo", "So at least this story was circulating in the second century. Whether the whole collection existed that early is still debated.", "tisch_242"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- V
 ("chapter", "V|The Carpenter's Son", "tis_77"),
 ("vo", "After the teacher scene, the tone begins to change. Jesus laughs, and everyone he had cursed is made whole again.", "tis_77"),
 ("vo", "Now the miracles are mostly kind, and very domestic. A playmate named Zeno falls from a roof and dies. When the parents blame Jesus, he raises the boy, who tells everyone, you did not throw me down.", "rh_137"),
 ("vo", "His mother sends him to the well. The pitcher breaks, so he carries the water home in his cloak.", "rh_134"),
 ("vo", "He sows a single grain of wheat, and reaps enough to feed all the poor of the village.", "rh_131"),
 ("vo", "Joseph, a carpenter, cuts one beam of a rich man's bed too short. The boy takes hold of it, and stretches it to the right length.", "tis_38"),
 ("vo", "And when his brother James is bitten by a viper while gathering wood, Jesus blows on the bite, and James is healed.", "tis_77"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- VI
 ("chapter", "VI|Twelve Years Old", "tis_74"),
 ("vo", "The book ends where Luke's Gospel picks up the thread. At twelve, Jesus stays behind in Jerusalem after Passover. His parents search for three days, and find him in the Temple among the teachers.", "tis_71"),
 ("vo", "In this telling, he is silencing the elders, explaining the law and the parables of the prophets. Then he goes home with his parents, and is obedient to them.", "tis_72"),
 ("vo", "The fierce child of the opening pages has grown into the Jesus the Gospels know. Some scholars think that arc, from danger to wisdom, is the point of the book.", "tis_74"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- VII
 ("chapter", "VII|Not That Gospel of Thomas", "wr_133"),
 ("vo", "One warning. This is not the Gospel of Thomas found at Nag Hammadi in 1945, the collection of a hundred and fourteen sayings. The two books are completely different.", "wr_133"),
 ("vo", "In fact, the earliest copies of the infancy stories carry no author's name at all. Some medieval Greek manuscripts open with a line from Thomas the Israelite, and the name stuck.", "wr_127"),
 ("vo", "The stories were translated into Syriac, Latin, Georgian, Ethiopic, Slavonic, Arabic and Irish. A Syriac copy in the British Library dates to the sixth century. The scholar William Wright first printed it in 1865.", "wr_133"),
 ("vo", "In the West, many of the stories were folded into a later Latin book about Mary and the childhood of Jesus, known today as the Gospel of Pseudo Matthew.", "dore_057"),
 ("vo", "Around the year thirteen hundred, an English craftsman scratched them into clay tiles. They are linked to the church at Tring, in Hertfordshire, and are now in the British Museum, and the Victoria and Albert Museum in London.", "rh_137"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- VIII
 ("chapter", "VIII|A Scrap in Hamburg", "tisch_242"),
 ("vo", "For a long time, the oldest Greek copy known was from the eleventh century. Then, in June 2024, two papyrologists, Lajos Berkes and Gabriel Nocchi Macedo, announced a find in the state and university library of Hamburg.", "tisch_242"),
 ("vo", "It is a fragment from Egypt, about eleven centimeters by five, with thirteen lines of Greek. Because the handwriting was so clumsy, it had been taken for an everyday document, like a private letter.", "tis_64"),
 ("vo", "Then Berkes noticed a word: Jesus. Letter by letter, the two scholars matched it to the opening of the sparrow story.", "dore_067"),
 ("vo", "They date it to the fourth or fifth century, centuries older than any other Greek copy. Because the hand is so unsteady, they suggest it may have been a writing exercise, copied by a student in a school or a monastery.", "tisch_241"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- IX
 ("chapter", "IX|What Was It For?", "dore_062"),
 ("vo", "Not everyone in the early church liked these stories. In the fourth century, Epiphanius of Salamis was willing to accept that Jesus worked wonders as a child. But John Chrysostom, preaching in Antioch, was blunt.", "dore_062"),
 ("quote", "The miracles which they say belong to Christ's Childhood, are false, and the inventions of certain who bring them into notice.|John Chrysostom, Homilies on the Gospel of St. John 17 (Library of the Fathers, Oxford, 1848)", "dore_062"),
 ("vo", "His reason was simple: the Gospel of John says Jesus did his first miracle at a wedding in Cana. A sixth century Latin list, known as the Gelasian Decree, names a book of the infancy of the Saviour among the books not to be received.", "dore_066"),
 ("vo", "Yet copies kept being made, and the stories kept being told. Modern scholars read them in different ways.", "tis_77"),
 ("vo", "Some, like Tony Burke, point out that ancient biographies of heroes and kings loved stories of a gifted, dangerous child. Others, like Reidar Aasgaard, read it as a story for ordinary Christian households, where children quarrel, parents worry, and in the end all is well.", "tis_74"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CLOSE
 ("chapter", "|A Childhood Imagined", "dore_061"),
 ("vo", "So, what is the Infancy Gospel of Thomas? A folk tale, a school text, or an early attempt to imagine God as a child? Perhaps it was all three.", "dore_061"),
 ("vo", "The Gospel of John gives its own answer to when the wonders began. This beginning of miracles did Jesus in Cana of Galilee, and manifested forth his glory.", "dore_059"),
 ("vo", "But for more than a thousand years, in churches and homes from Ireland to Ethiopia, people kept telling stories of the boy by the stream, and the sparrows that flew away.", "dore_067"),
 ("vo", "There are other lost books. The Ethiopian Bible, with eighty one books. The Book of the Wars of the Lord, which the Bible cites by name. And the Protevangelium of James, the story of Mary's own childhood.", "rh_131"),
 ("vo", "If you want to hear their stories, subscribe, and tell us in the comments which one we should open next.", "rh_131"),
 ("end", "THE LOST SCRIPTURES", "rh_131"),
]
